' dom/layout — compute each element's flex geometry from its resolved Style.
'
' After resolveStyles() has filled every ElementNode.style,
' updateLayout(doc, viewportPx, cellPx, linePx, fitWidth) walks the tree and fills
' each ElementNode.layout (and each TextNode.layout): an absolute box
' (x, y, width, height) in device-independent **CSS px** from the viewport's
' top-left. A display:flex box uses flex layout along its flex-direction; every
' other box uses block formatting (block children stack, inline children flow into
' wrapped runs); display:none is a zero box.
'
' The DOM is device-independent: it lays out in CSS px and never knows about
' terminal cells — a text renderer (display::) or a future pixel renderer maps the
' px geometry to its own grid. The one thing layout needs from the renderer is the
' glyph size in px (`cellPx` wide, `linePx` tall for a monospace font), so a text
' run's px extent is its column count times cellPx and its px height is its line
' count times linePx. `fitWidth` treats an explicit width wider than the viewport as
' auto (fill), so a desktop-width page reflows to a narrow viewport instead of
' overflowing. Layout depends on the viewport and glyph size, so it is recomputed on
' each render/resize rather than stored once by the worker.
'
' Like resolve, this lives in dom so it can recurse over dom's OWN Node union
' (native lowering is fine inside the defining package); a consumer that imported
' Node would have to iterate.
'
' Supported subset (a terminal text browser, not a conformant CSS engine):
'   - main axis: flex-direction row / column (reverse falls back to forward)
'   - flex items: flex-grow, flex-shrink, flex-basis, width, height
'   - box model: padding + margin on all four sides, gap between items
'   - justify-content on the main axis (all six keywords)
'   - single line only (flex-wrap is not honored yet; every line is nowrap)
'   - cross axis: children start at the cross origin; column children stretch to
'     the content width, row children keep their measured height (no align yet)
' Text is measured by strings::displayWidth (columns) times the glyph width, and
' wrapped to the available px width to size a leaf box's height.

IMPORT collections
IMPORT strings

' The computed geometry of one element: an absolute border box in **CSS px**,
' (x, y) is the top-left corner measured from the viewport origin; width/height is
' the border box (content + padding; there are no borders). Margins position the
' box within its parent but are not part of these dimensions.
EXPORT TYPE Layout
  x AS Integer
  y AS Integer
  width AS Integer
  height AS Integer
END TYPE

' The initial (unlaid) geometry: a zero box at the origin. The parser and style
' resolution leave every element here; updateLayout fills it in.
EXPORT FUNC zeroLayout() AS Layout
  RETURN Layout[0, 0, 0, 0]
END FUNC

' The width prepass, run once before layout. It (1) resolves a viewport-relative
' width to px — the viewport is fixed for the whole pass, so `60vw` becomes
' 60/100 * viewportPx here; a `vh`/`vmin`/`vmax` width has no viewport height in a
' scrolling text viewport, so it falls back to auto — and (2) applies fitWidth: an
' explicit px width wider than the viewport becomes auto (fill) so a desktop-width
' page reflows to a small viewport instead of overflowing off the edge. A percent
' width is left tagged for layout, which resolves it against the containing block.
' Recurses over dom's own Node union (native inside dom); text nodes are unchanged.
FUNC resolveWidths(n AS Node, viewportPx AS Integer, fitWidth AS Boolean) AS Node
  MATCH n
    CASE ElementNode(e)
      MUT kids AS List OF Node = []
      FOR EACH c IN e.children
        kids = collections::append(kids, resolveWidths(c, viewportPx, fitWidth))
      NEXT
      MUT st AS Style = e.style
      IF st.width >= 0 THEN
        IF st.widthUnit = LengthUnit.Vw THEN
          st = WITH st { width := st.width * viewportPx / 100 }
          st = WITH st { widthUnit := LengthUnit.Px }
        ELSEIF st.widthUnit = LengthUnit.Vh OR st.widthUnit = LengthUnit.Vmin OR st.widthUnit = LengthUnit.Vmax THEN
          st = WITH st { width := -1 }
        END IF
      END IF
      IF fitWidth AND st.widthUnit = LengthUnit.Px AND st.width > viewportPx THEN
        st = WITH st { width := -1 }
      END IF
      RETURN elementStyled(e.tag, e.attrs, kids, st)
    CASE ELSE
      RETURN n
  END MATCH
END FUNC

' The resolved px width of an element, or -1 for auto, given the width available to
' it (its containing block's content width, for a percent). Viewport units are
' already px by the time layout runs (resolveWidths), so only Percent needs the
' containing block here.
FUNC resolveWidthPx(st AS Style, availW AS Integer) AS Integer
  IF st.width < 0 THEN RETURN -1
  IF st.widthUnit = LengthUnit.Percent THEN RETURN st.width * availW / 100
  RETURN st.width
END FUNC

' ----- internal results ---------------------------------------------------------

' One node laid out: the rebuilt node plus its OUTER (margin box) size, which is
' what a parent's flow advances by.
TYPE Sized
  node AS Node
  width AS Integer
  height AS Integer
END TYPE

' The result of laying out a container's children: the rebuilt child list and the
' content box extent they occupied.
TYPE Flow
  kids AS List OF Node
  usedW AS Integer
  usedH AS Integer
END TYPE

' ----- small numeric + text helpers --------------------------------------------

FUNC maxI(a AS Integer, b AS Integer) AS Integer
  IF a > b THEN RETURN a
  RETURN b
END FUNC

FUNC minI(a AS Integer, b AS Integer) AS Integer
  IF a < b THEN RETURN a
  RETURN b
END FUNC

FUNC sumList(xs AS List OF Integer) AS Integer
  MUT total AS Integer = 0
  FOR EACH x IN xs
    total = total + x
  NEXT
  RETURN total
END FUNC

' Collapse every run of whitespace to a single space and trim, matching how the
' renderer flows inline text — so measurement lines up with what is drawn.
FUNC collapseWs(s AS String) AS String
  MUT t AS String = strings::replace(s, "\n", " ")
  t = strings::replace(t, "\t", " ")
  t = strings::replace(t, "\r", " ")
  MUT words AS List OF String = []
  FOR EACH w IN strings::split(t, " ")
    IF w <> "" THEN words = collections::append(words, w)
  NEXT
  RETURN strings::join(words, " ")
END FUNC

' The one-line px width of a text run: its column count times the glyph width `cw`.
FUNC textWidth(s AS String, cw AS Integer) AS Integer
  RETURN strings::displayWidth(collapseWs(s)) * cw
END FUNC

' The px height a text run occupies when word-wrapped to `widthPx` px: its line count
' (each `linePx` tall) at a wrap boundary of widthPx/cw columns (matching display::wrap:
' a single word wider than the box is left whole).
FUNC textHeight(s AS String, widthPx AS Integer, cw AS Integer, linePx AS Integer) AS Integer
  LET t AS String = collapseWs(s)
  IF t = "" THEN RETURN 0
  LET w AS Integer = maxI(1, widthPx / cw)
  MUT lines AS Integer = 1
  MUT cur AS Integer = 0
  FOR EACH word IN strings::split(t, " ")
    LET ww AS Integer = strings::displayWidth(word)
    IF cur = 0 THEN
      cur = ww
    ELSEIF cur + 1 + ww <= w THEN
      cur = cur + 1 + ww
    ELSE
      lines = lines + 1
      cur = ww
    END IF
  NEXT
  RETURN lines * linePx
END FUNC

' ----- per-child flex inputs ---------------------------------------------------

FUNC isNone(n AS Node) AS Boolean
  MATCH n
    CASE ElementNode(e)
      RETURN e.style.display = Display.None
    CASE ELSE
      RETURN FALSE
  END MATCH
END FUNC

FUNC childGrow(n AS Node) AS Integer
  MATCH n
    CASE ElementNode(e)
      RETURN maxI(0, e.style.flexGrow)
    CASE ELSE
      RETURN 0
  END MATCH
END FUNC

FUNC childShrink(n AS Node) AS Integer
  MATCH n
    CASE ElementNode(e)
      RETURN maxI(0, e.style.flexShrink)
    CASE ELSE
      RETURN 1
  END MATCH
END FUNC

' The natural (pre-grow/shrink) main-axis OUTER size of a child (its border box
' plus horizontal margins): flex-basis if set, else an explicit width, else its
' max-content width. Text measures to its one-line display width, capped at the
' available width so one very long word never forces the whole line to overflow.
FUNC childBaseMain(n AS Node, contentW AS Integer, cw AS Integer) AS Integer
  MATCH n
    CASE ElementNode(e)
      IF e.style.flexBasis >= 0 THEN RETURN e.style.flexBasis + maxI(0, e.style.marginLeft) + maxI(0, e.style.marginRight)
      LET wpx AS Integer = resolveWidthPx(e.style, contentW)
      IF wpx >= 0 THEN RETURN wpx + maxI(0, e.style.marginLeft) + maxI(0, e.style.marginRight)
      RETURN maxContentOuterW(n, cw)
    CASE TextNode(t)
      RETURN minI(contentW, textWidth(t.text, cw))
    CASE ELSE
      RETURN 0
  END MATCH
END FUNC

' The max-content OUTER width of a node: how wide it wants to be with unlimited
' room. Structural (no trial layout, no fill): a leaf's is its text width; a
' container's is the row-sum or column-max of its children's max-content widths.
' Used to give an auto-sized row item its natural base size.
FUNC maxContentOuterW(n AS Node, cw AS Integer) AS Integer
  MATCH n
    CASE ElementNode(e)
      RETURN elementMaxContentOuterW(e, cw)
    CASE TextNode(t)
      RETURN textWidth(t.text, cw)
    CASE ELSE
      RETURN 0
  END MATCH
END FUNC

FUNC elementMaxContentOuterW(e AS ElementNode, cw AS Integer) AS Integer
  LET st AS Style = e.style
  IF st.display = Display.None THEN RETURN 0
  LET ml AS Integer = maxI(0, st.marginLeft)
  LET mr AS Integer = maxI(0, st.marginRight)
  LET pl AS Integer = maxI(0, st.paddingLeft)
  LET pr AS Integer = maxI(0, st.paddingRight)
  ' A fixed px width pins max-content; a percent width has no fixed max-content
  ' (there is no containing block here), so fall through to the content-based size.
  IF st.width >= 0 AND st.widthUnit = LengthUnit.Px THEN RETURN st.width + ml + mr
  MUT inner AS Integer = 0
  LET rowish AS Boolean = st.display = Display.Flex AND (st.direction = FlexDirection.Row OR st.direction = FlexDirection.RowReverse)
  IF rowish THEN
    ' a flex row: children lay side by side, plus the gaps
    LET gap AS Integer = maxI(0, st.gap)
    MUT n AS Integer = 0
    FOR EACH k IN e.children
      IF isNone(k) = FALSE THEN
        inner = inner + maxContentOuterW(k, cw)
        n = n + 1
      END IF
    NEXT
    inner = inner + gap * maxI(0, n - 1)
  ELSEIF hasBlockChild(e) THEN
    ' a column or block with block children: the widest child wins
    FOR EACH k IN e.children
      inner = maxI(inner, maxContentOuterW(k, cw))
    NEXT
  ELSE
    ' inline content: it flows into one run, so its natural width is the whole
    ' decorated text on one line (gatherChildren includes [link]/<button>/|field|
    ' decoration, which is what actually paints — measuring the raw text undersizes
    ' the box and clips the brackets).
    inner = textWidth(gatherChildren(e), cw)
  END IF
  RETURN maxI(0, inner) + pl + pr + ml + mr
END FUNC

' ----- main-axis size distribution (flex-grow / flex-shrink) --------------------

' Given each item's base main size and its grow/shrink factors, return the final
' main sizes after distributing the free space `free` (positive: grow; negative:
' shrink). Rounding remainder goes to the first growing/shrinking item so the line
' fills the container exactly. A shrinking item never drops below its `mins` entry
' (its min-content width) — real flexbox's automatic minimum size — so items overflow
' (and clip at the edge) rather than collapsing to a column of single characters.
FUNC distributeMain(bases AS List OF Integer, grows AS List OF Integer, shrinks AS List OF Integer, mins AS List OF Integer, free AS Integer) AS List OF Integer
  LET n AS Integer = len(bases)
  MUT finals AS List OF Integer = []
  IF n = 0 THEN RETURN finals

  IF free >= 0 THEN
    LET sumGrow AS Integer = sumList(grows)
    IF sumGrow <= 0 THEN
      FOR EACH b IN bases
        finals = collections::append(finals, b)
      NEXT
      RETURN finals
    END IF
    MUT adds AS List OF Integer = []
    MUT i AS Integer = 0
    DO WHILE i < n
      adds = collections::append(adds, (free * collections::get(grows, i)) / sumGrow)
      i = i + 1
    LOOP
    MUT leftover AS Integer = free - sumList(adds)
    MUT given AS Boolean = FALSE
    i = 0
    DO WHILE i < n
      MUT extra AS Integer = 0
      IF given = FALSE AND collections::get(grows, i) > 0 THEN
        extra = leftover
        given = TRUE
      END IF
      finals = collections::append(finals, collections::get(bases, i) + collections::get(adds, i) + extra)
      i = i + 1
    LOOP
    RETURN finals
  END IF

  ' free < 0: shrink, weighted by shrink-factor * base size.
  LET deficit AS Integer = -free
  MUT sumW AS Integer = 0
  MUT j AS Integer = 0
  DO WHILE j < n
    sumW = sumW + collections::get(shrinks, j) * collections::get(bases, j)
    j = j + 1
  LOOP
  IF sumW <= 0 THEN
    FOR EACH b IN bases
      finals = collections::append(finals, b)
    NEXT
    RETURN finals
  END IF
  MUT cuts AS List OF Integer = []
  MUT k AS Integer = 0
  DO WHILE k < n
    LET w AS Integer = collections::get(shrinks, k) * collections::get(bases, k)
    cuts = collections::append(cuts, (deficit * w) / sumW)
    k = k + 1
  LOOP
  MUT leftDef AS Integer = deficit - sumList(cuts)
  MUT gave AS Boolean = FALSE
  k = 0
  DO WHILE k < n
    MUT c AS Integer = collections::get(cuts, k)
    IF gave = FALSE AND collections::get(shrinks, k) > 0 AND collections::get(bases, k) > 0 THEN
      c = c + leftDef
      gave = TRUE
    END IF
    finals = collections::append(finals, maxI(collections::get(mins, k), collections::get(bases, k) - c))
    k = k + 1
  LOOP
  RETURN finals
END FUNC

' ----- laying out a container's children ---------------------------------------

' A child is block-level when it is an element that lays out as its own box on its
' own line (display:block or display:flex). Text and inline elements are inline-
' level and flow together into line boxes.
FUNC isBlockLevel(n AS Node) AS Boolean
  MATCH n
    CASE ElementNode(e)
      ' Decorated controls always flow inline so they keep their glyphs ([link],
      ' <button>, |field|): a real page may set display:block on a link, but as a
      ' text control it still reads best inline and bracketed, not as a bare block.
      IF e.tag = "a" OR e.tag = "button" OR e.tag = "input" THEN RETURN FALSE
      IF e.style.display = Display.Block OR e.style.display = Display.Flex THEN RETURN TRUE
      ' <br> is a forced line break: treating it as a (zero-height) block flushes
      ' the inline run before it, so the content after it starts a new line.
      IF e.tag = "br" THEN RETURN TRUE
      ' Block-in-inline: a nominally inline element that actually wraps block-level
      ' content (common in real pages — a <span> or <center> around <div>s) must
      ' break out as a block, or its blocks get flattened into one crammed run.
      RETURN hasBlockChild(e)
    CASE ELSE
      RETURN FALSE
  END MATCH
END FUNC

' Whether any descendant of `e` is block-level (drives the block-in-inline rule).
FUNC hasBlockChild(e AS ElementNode) AS Boolean
  FOR EACH c IN e.children
    IF isBlockLevel(c) THEN RETURN TRUE
  NEXT
  RETURN FALSE
END FUNC

' An HTML attribute parsed as a positive Integer (its `size`/`rows`/`cols` count),
' or `dflt` when absent or non-numeric.
FUNC attrInt(attrs AS Map OF String TO String, key AS String, dflt AS Integer) AS Integer
  LET raw AS String = strings::trim(collections::getOr(attrs, key, ""))
  IF raw = "" THEN RETURN dflt
  LET v AS Integer = toInt(raw, 10) TRAP(e)
    RECOVER dflt
  END TRAP
  RETURN maxI(1, v)
END FUNC

' The `type` of an <input>, lower-cased and defaulting to "text".
FUNC inputType(e AS ElementNode) AS String
  RETURN strings::lower(strings::trim(collections::getOr(e.attrs, "type", "text")))
END FUNC

' Whether an <input> is a text-entry field the reader can type into — everything
' that renders as a |S:| / |N:| box. The push-button types (submit/reset/button),
' the toggles (checkbox/radio) and a hidden field have no typed value, so they are
' not editable and never become focus targets.
FUNC isEditableInput(e AS ElementNode) AS Boolean
  IF e.tag <> "input" THEN RETURN FALSE
  LET t AS String = inputType(e)
  RETURN NOT (t = "hidden" OR t = "submit" OR t = "reset" OR t = "button" OR t = "checkbox" OR t = "radio" OR t = "image")
END FUNC

' The attribute indexFields stamps an editable <input> with: its ordinal in document
' order. It is the field's identity everywhere — the inline gather tags the box's
' glyphs with it, the renderer exposes it as a focus target, and applyFieldValues
' places the reader's value by it. A synthetic attribute (no page uses this name), so
' the ordinal travels with the element through every tree rebuild for free.
FUNC fieldAttr() AS String
  RETURN "mfb-field"
END FUNC

' The field ordinal stamped on an element by indexFields, or -1 when it carries none
' (every element that is not an editable <input>, and any document that was never
' indexed).
FUNC fieldOrdinalOf(e AS ElementNode) AS Integer
  LET raw AS String = collections::getOr(e.attrs, fieldAttr(), "")
  IF raw = "" THEN RETURN -1
  LET v AS Integer = toInt(raw, 10) TRAP(err)
    RECOVER -1
  END TRAP
  RETURN v
END FUNC

' The last `n` display columns of `s` (whole graphemes). A field shows the tail of
' an over-long value, so the text being typed stays visible at the box's right edge.
FUNC tailCols(s AS String, n AS Integer) AS String
  IF n <= 0 THEN RETURN ""
  LET gs AS List OF String = strings::graphemes(s)
  MUT out AS String = ""
  MUT used AS Integer = 0
  MUT i AS Integer = len(gs) - 1
  DO WHILE i >= 0
    LET g AS String = collections::get(gs, i)
    LET gw AS Integer = strings::displayWidth(g)
    IF used + gw > n THEN EXIT DO
    out = g & out
    used = used + gw
    i = i - 1
  LOOP
  RETURN out
END FUNC

' A field's value as it is drawn inside its box: `n` columns wide, the value's tail
' when it overflows, then "-" filler for the unused cells.
'
' Every space becomes a non-breaking space (U+00A0). The inline gather collapses
' whitespace runs and the renderer word-wraps on U+0020, so a real space inside a
' field would shrink the box and could break it across two lines; U+00A0 is one
' column wide, renders as a space, and survives both. It is a display detail only —
' the value the app stores keeps ordinary spaces.
FUNC fieldContent(raw AS String, n AS Integer) AS String
  LET shown AS String = strings::replace(tailCols(raw, n), " ", "\u{A0}")
  LET w AS Integer = strings::displayWidth(shown)
  IF w >= n THEN RETURN shown
  RETURN shown & strings::repeat("-", n - w)
END FUNC

' The visible glyph an <input> renders as, so the field shows in the flowed text.
' A single-line text field is |S:----|, a number field |N:----| and a password field
' |P:****| — the box spans its `size` columns and shows the field's current value
' (typed by the reader, or supplied by the page) followed by "-" filler. A password's
' value is masked; a submit/reset/button/image becomes a <label>; check/radio are
' [ ]/( ); a hidden field renders nothing.
FUNC inputBox(e AS ElementNode) AS String
  LET t AS String = inputType(e)
  IF t = "submit" THEN RETURN "<" & collections::getOr(e.attrs, "value", "Submit") & ">"
  IF t = "reset" THEN RETURN "<" & collections::getOr(e.attrs, "value", "Reset") & ">"
  IF t = "button" THEN RETURN "<" & collections::getOr(e.attrs, "value", "Button") & ">"
  ' A type=image is a submit button too, labelled by its alt text — not the text box
  ' the fall-through below would otherwise draw for an unrecognised type.
  IF t = "image" THEN RETURN "<" & collections::getOr(e.attrs, "alt", "Submit") & ">"
  RETURN inputBoxOf(t, attrInt(e.attrs, "size", 20), collections::getOr(e.attrs, "value", ""))
END FUNC

' The box an editable field of type `kind` and width `size` draws when it holds
' `value` — the one place a field's appearance is decided, so the full layout pass and
' an app redrawing a single field in place cannot render it differently. The push
' buttons are inputBox's business (their label is an attribute, not reader data).
FUNC inputBoxOf(kind AS String, size AS Integer, value AS String) AS String
  IF kind = "hidden" THEN RETURN ""
  IF kind = "checkbox" THEN RETURN "[ ]"
  IF kind = "radio" THEN RETURN "( )"
  LET n AS Integer = maxI(1, size)
  IF kind = "password" THEN
    RETURN "|P:" & fieldContent(strings::repeat("*", len(strings::graphemes(value))), n) & "|"
  END IF
  IF kind = "number" THEN RETURN "|N:" & fieldContent(value, n) & "|"
  RETURN "|S:" & fieldContent(value, n) & "|"
END FUNC

' Concatenate the visible text of an inline subtree, decorating the controls that
' would otherwise be invisible so the reader can see them: a link's text is wrapped
' in [brackets], a <button>'s label in <angle brackets>, and an <input> becomes a
' field glyph (see inputBox). Other inline elements (<b>, <em>, <span>…) are
' transparent, and a display:none subtree contributes nothing. Used to flatten a run
' of inline content into one flowed text run.
' Wrap an element's inline content in `open`/`close`, or render nothing when the
' content is empty (so an icon-only link is not a bare "[]"). A top-level helper
' because a LET-bound call inside a MATCH CASE mis-types to Unknown at codegen.
FUNC wrapChildren(e AS ElementNode, open AS String, close AS String) AS String
  LET inner AS String = gatherChildren(e)
  IF strings::trim(inner) = "" THEN RETURN ""
  RETURN open & inner & close
END FUNC

FUNC gatherText(n AS Node) AS String
  MATCH n
    CASE ElementNode(e)
      IF e.style.display = Display.None THEN RETURN ""
      IF e.tag = "a" THEN RETURN wrapChildren(e, "[", "]")
      IF e.tag = "button" THEN RETURN wrapChildren(e, "<", ">")
      IF e.tag = "input" THEN RETURN inputBox(e)
      RETURN gatherChildren(e)
    CASE TextNode(t)
      RETURN t.text
    CASE ELSE
      RETURN ""
  END MATCH
END FUNC

FUNC gatherChildren(e AS ElementNode) AS String
  MUT s AS String = ""
  FOR EACH c IN e.children
    s = s & gatherText(c)
  NEXT
  RETURN s
END FUNC

' ----- attributed inline gather (the styled twin of gatherText) ----------------
'
' The renderer wants each run's text plus which stretches are a link / bold /
' underline, so it can colour and style them. gatherAttributed walks the same inline
' subtree gatherText does and produces the same characters, but as a list of Glyph
' (one per grapheme) each tagged with the inline style in effect: a link is
' link-coloured and underlined (and bracketed), <b>/<strong> is bold, <u>/<ins> is
' underlined; a <button> is angle-bracketed and an <input> is its field glyph. The
' glyphs are then collapsed (matching collapseWs) and reduced to TextSpans over the
' collapsed text — so the spans line up with what the renderer wraps and draws.

' The link foreground colour, packed 0xRRGGBB (a readable blue on a dark terminal).
FUNC linkColor() AS Integer
  RETURN 90 * 65536 + 160 * 256 + 255
END FUNC

' One styled grapheme of an inline run. `href` is the link URL when this glyph is part
' of a hyperlink's text (empty otherwise) — a link's [brackets] carry the link style
' but an empty href, so a link's target covers its text only. `field` is the form-field
' ordinal when the glyph is part of an editable <input>'s box, -1 otherwise.
TYPE Glyph
  ch AS String
  bold AS Boolean
  underline AS Boolean
  color AS Integer
  href AS String
  field AS Integer
END TYPE

' Split a plain string into styled glyphs, one per grapheme, all carrying
' (b, u, c, href, field).
FUNC styleGlyphs(s AS String, b AS Boolean, u AS Boolean, c AS Integer, href AS String, field AS Integer) AS List OF Glyph
  MUT out AS List OF Glyph = []
  FOR EACH g IN strings::graphemes(s)
    out = collections::append(out, Glyph[g, b, u, c, href, field])
  NEXT
  RETURN out
END FUNC

FUNC gatherChildrenGlyphs(e AS ElementNode, b AS Boolean, u AS Boolean, c AS Integer, href AS String) AS List OF Glyph
  MUT out AS List OF Glyph = []
  FOR EACH k IN e.children
    FOR EACH g IN gatherAttributed(k, b, u, c, href)
      out = collections::append(out, g)
    NEXT
  NEXT
  RETURN out
END FUNC

' Wrap an element's styled inline content in open/close glyphs (the same [ ]/< >
' decoration gatherText adds), or nothing when the content is blank. The inner content
' carries `innerHref` and the brackets `bracketHref`: a link passes its URL as the
' inner href and "" for the brackets, so its navigation target is the text only.
FUNC wrapGlyphs(e AS ElementNode, open AS String, close AS String, b AS Boolean, u AS Boolean, c AS Integer, innerHref AS String, bracketHref AS String) AS List OF Glyph
  LET inner AS List OF Glyph = gatherChildrenGlyphs(e, b, u, c, innerHref)
  MUT joined AS String = ""
  FOR EACH g IN inner
    joined = joined & g.ch
  NEXT
  IF strings::trim(joined) = "" THEN RETURN []
  MUT out AS List OF Glyph = [Glyph[open, b, u, c, bracketHref, -1]]
  FOR EACH g IN inner
    out = collections::append(out, g)
  NEXT
  out = collections::append(out, Glyph[close, b, u, c, bracketHref, -1])
  RETURN out
END FUNC

FUNC gatherAttributed(n AS Node, b AS Boolean, u AS Boolean, c AS Integer, href AS String) AS List OF Glyph
  MATCH n
    CASE ElementNode(e)
      IF e.style.display = Display.None THEN RETURN []
      IF e.tag = "a" THEN RETURN wrapGlyphs(e, "[", "]", b, TRUE, linkColor(), collections::getOr(e.attrs, "href", ""), "")
      IF e.tag = "button" THEN RETURN wrapGlyphs(e, "<", ">", b, u, c, href, href)
      IF e.tag = "input" THEN RETURN styleGlyphs(inputBox(e), b, u, c, href, fieldOrdinalOf(e))
      IF e.tag = "b" OR e.tag = "strong" THEN RETURN gatherChildrenGlyphs(e, TRUE, u, c, href)
      IF e.tag = "u" OR e.tag = "ins" THEN RETURN gatherChildrenGlyphs(e, b, TRUE, c, href)
      RETURN gatherChildrenGlyphs(e, b, u, c, href)
    CASE TextNode(t)
      RETURN styleGlyphs(t.text, b, u, c, href, -1)
    CASE ELSE
      RETURN []
  END MATCH
END FUNC

' Whether two glyphs carry the same terminal style, link and form field.
FUNC sameStyle(a AS Glyph, g AS Glyph) AS Boolean
  RETURN a.bold = g.bold AND a.underline = g.underline AND a.color = g.color AND a.href = g.href AND a.field = g.field
END FUNC

' Collapse whitespace runs to single spaces and trim, exactly as collapseWs does for
' plain text (so a run's measured line count matches). A collapsed space carries the
' style/href of the first whitespace glyph it replaces, so a space *inside* a styled
' element (a link's or <b>'s internal space) stays styled and the run stays contiguous.
FUNC collapseGlyphs(gs AS List OF Glyph) AS List OF Glyph
  MUT out AS List OF Glyph = []
  MUT pendingSpace AS Boolean = FALSE
  MUT spB AS Boolean = FALSE
  MUT spU AS Boolean = FALSE
  MUT spC AS Integer = -1
  MUT spHref AS String = ""
  MUT spField AS Integer = -1
  MUT started AS Boolean = FALSE
  FOR EACH g IN gs
    LET isWs AS Boolean = g.ch = " " OR g.ch = "\t" OR g.ch = "\n" OR g.ch = "\r"
    IF isWs THEN
      IF started AND NOT pendingSpace THEN
        pendingSpace = TRUE
        spB = g.bold
        spU = g.underline
        spC = g.color
        spHref = g.href
        spField = g.field
      END IF
    ELSE
      IF pendingSpace THEN
        out = collections::append(out, Glyph[" ", spB, spU, spC, spHref, spField])
        pendingSpace = FALSE
      END IF
      out = collections::append(out, g)
      started = TRUE
    END IF
  NEXT
  RETURN out
END FUNC

' The visible text of a collapsed glyph list.
FUNC glyphsText(gs AS List OF Glyph) AS String
  MUT s AS String = ""
  FOR EACH g IN gs
    s = s & g.ch
  NEXT
  RETURN s
END FUNC

' Reduce a collapsed glyph list to TextSpans over its grapheme indices: one span per
' maximal run of styled (non-default) glyphs. Default (plain) glyphs produce no span.
' An editable field's box is "styled" even when it carries no colour/bold/underline:
' its span is what exposes the field to the renderer as a focus target.
FUNC glyphsSpans(gs AS List OF Glyph) AS List OF TextSpan
  MUT spans AS List OF TextSpan = []
  MUT i AS Integer = 0
  LET n AS Integer = len(gs)
  DO WHILE i < n
    LET g AS Glyph = collections::get(gs, i)
    IF g.bold OR g.underline OR g.color >= 0 OR g.field >= 0 THEN
      MUT j AS Integer = i
      DO WHILE j + 1 < n AND sameStyle(collections::get(gs, j + 1), g)
        j = j + 1
      LOOP
      spans = collections::append(spans, TextSpan[i, j, g.bold, g.underline, g.color, g.href, g.field])
      i = j + 1
    ELSE
      i = i + 1
    END IF
  LOOP
  RETURN spans
END FUNC

' Lay a run of inline-level nodes as one flowed text box at (x, y), wrapping to the
' content width and taking its line alignment from the block's text-align. Gathers
' the run's styled glyphs, collapses them, and carries the resulting text + spans so
' the renderer can colour/style it. Returns the run (empty height if it has no text).
FUNC inlineRun(pending AS List OF Node, st AS Style, x AS Integer, y AS Integer, contentW AS Integer, cw AS Integer, linePx AS Integer) AS Sized
  MUT raw AS List OF Glyph = []
  FOR EACH k IN pending
    FOR EACH g IN gatherAttributed(k, FALSE, FALSE, -1, "")
      raw = collections::append(raw, g)
    NEXT
  NEXT
  LET glyphs AS List OF Glyph = collapseGlyphs(raw)
  LET text AS String = glyphsText(glyphs)
  LET spans AS List OF TextSpan = glyphsSpans(glyphs)
  LET h AS Integer = textHeight(text, contentW, cw, linePx)
  LET box AS Layout = Layout[x, y, contentW, h]
  RETURN Sized[textLaid(text, box, st.textAlign, spans), contentW, h]
END FUNC

' Shift a laid subtree horizontally by dx: every box (element and text) moves right
' by dx. Used to centre or right-align a block child after it has been laid out.
FUNC translateNode(n AS Node, dx AS Integer) AS Node
  MATCH n
    CASE ElementNode(e)
      MUT kids AS List OF Node = []
      FOR EACH c IN e.children
        kids = collections::append(kids, translateNode(c, dx))
      NEXT
      LET b AS Layout = e.layout
      RETURN elementLaid(e.tag, e.attrs, kids, e.style, Layout[b.x + dx, b.y, b.width, b.height])
    CASE TextNode(t)
      LET b AS Layout = t.layout
      RETURN textLaid(t.text, Layout[b.x + dx, b.y, b.width, b.height], t.align, t.spans)
    CASE ELSE
      RETURN n
  END MATCH
END FUNC

' Position a just-laid block child horizontally within its container per the
' container's text-align: a child narrower than the content box is centred or
' right-aligned (a full-width child is untouched). This is how a shrink-to-fit
' block — a <table>, or a fixed-width element — is centred by a <center> /
' text-align:center ancestor.
FUNC alignBlockChild(node AS Node, childW AS Integer, contentW AS Integer, align AS Justify) AS Node
  IF childW >= contentW THEN RETURN node
  MUT dx AS Integer = 0
  IF align = Justify.Center THEN dx = (contentW - childW) / 2
  IF align = Justify.FlexEnd THEN dx = contentW - childW
  IF dx <= 0 THEN RETURN node
  RETURN translateNode(node, dx)
END FUNC

' Block formatting: stack the children vertically, each filling the content width.
' Consecutive inline-level children (text + inline elements) between block-level
' children are flowed together into one wrapped run (an anonymous block); block-
' level children lay out as their own boxes, and a narrower-than-full block (a
' shrink-to-fit table, a fixed-width element) is centred/right-aligned per the
' container's text-align. Vertical spacing comes from each box's margins, not a gap.
FUNC layoutBlock(kids AS List OF Node, st AS Style, originX AS Integer, originY AS Integer, contentW AS Integer, cw AS Integer, linePx AS Integer) AS Flow
  MUT outKids AS List OF Node = []
  MUT pending AS List OF Node = []
  MUT curY AS Integer = originY
  FOR EACH k IN kids
    IF isNone(k) THEN
      LET rn AS Sized = layoutNode(k, originX, curY, 0, FALSE, cw, linePx)
      outKids = collections::append(outKids, rn.node)
    ELSEIF isBlockLevel(k) THEN
      IF len(pending) > 0 THEN
        LET run AS Sized = inlineRun(pending, st, originX, curY, contentW, cw, linePx)
        IF run.height > 0 THEN
          outKids = collections::append(outKids, run.node)
          curY = curY + run.height
        END IF
        pending = []
      END IF
      LET r AS Sized = layoutNode(k, originX, curY, contentW, FALSE, cw, linePx)
      outKids = collections::append(outKids, alignBlockChild(r.node, r.width, contentW, st.textAlign))
      curY = curY + r.height
    ELSE
      pending = collections::append(pending, k)
    END IF
  NEXT
  IF len(pending) > 0 THEN
    LET run AS Sized = inlineRun(pending, st, originX, curY, contentW, cw, linePx)
    IF run.height > 0 THEN
      outKids = collections::append(outKids, run.node)
      curY = curY + run.height
    END IF
  END IF
  RETURN Flow[outKids, contentW, curY - originY]
END FUNC

' Column: stack children vertically, each stretched to the content width, with a
' gap between visible items. Content height is the total stacked height.
FUNC layoutColumn(kids AS List OF Node, st AS Style, originX AS Integer, originY AS Integer, contentW AS Integer, cw AS Integer, linePx AS Integer) AS Flow
  LET gap AS Integer = maxI(0, st.gap)
  MUT outKids AS List OF Node = []
  MUT curY AS Integer = originY
  MUT anyPlaced AS Boolean = FALSE
  FOR EACH k IN kids
    IF isNone(k) THEN
      LET rn AS Sized = layoutNode(k, originX, curY, 0, FALSE, cw, linePx)
      outKids = collections::append(outKids, rn.node)
    ELSE
      IF anyPlaced THEN curY = curY + gap
      ' Cross axis (width): self-size — explicit width honored, else stretch to fill.
      LET r AS Sized = layoutNode(k, originX, curY, contentW, FALSE, cw, linePx)
      outKids = collections::append(outKids, r.node)
      curY = curY + r.height
      anyPlaced = TRUE
    END IF
  NEXT
  RETURN Flow[outKids, contentW, curY - originY]
END FUNC

' Row: size items along the main (horizontal) axis with flex-grow/shrink/basis,
' distribute leftover space per justify-content, and place them left to right. The
' content height is the tallest item.
FUNC layoutRow(kids AS List OF Node, st AS Style, originX AS Integer, originY AS Integer, contentW AS Integer, cw AS Integer, linePx AS Integer) AS Flow
  LET gap AS Integer = maxI(0, st.gap)

  MUT bases AS List OF Integer = []
  MUT grows AS List OF Integer = []
  MUT shrinks AS List OF Integer = []
  MUT mins AS List OF Integer = []
  FOR EACH k IN kids
    IF isNone(k) = FALSE THEN
      LET base AS Integer = childBaseMain(k, contentW, cw)
      bases = collections::append(bases, base)
      grows = collections::append(grows, childGrow(k))
      shrinks = collections::append(shrinks, childShrink(k))
      ' Min-content, but never above the item's own base (a fixed/basis width caps it).
      mins = collections::append(mins, minI(base, minContentW(k, cw)))
    END IF
  NEXT
  LET nParts AS Integer = len(bases)
  LET gapsTotal AS Integer = gap * maxI(0, nParts - 1)
  LET free AS Integer = contentW - sumList(bases) - gapsTotal
  LET finals AS List OF Integer = distributeMain(bases, grows, shrinks, mins, free)

  LET usedMain AS Integer = sumList(finals) + gapsTotal
  LET leftover AS Integer = maxI(0, contentW - usedMain)
  MUT leadOffset AS Integer = 0
  MUT betweenExtra AS Integer = 0
  IF st.justify = Justify.FlexEnd THEN
    leadOffset = leftover
  ELSEIF st.justify = Justify.Center THEN
    leadOffset = leftover / 2
  ELSEIF st.justify = Justify.SpaceBetween THEN
    IF nParts > 1 THEN betweenExtra = leftover / (nParts - 1)
  ELSEIF st.justify = Justify.SpaceAround THEN
    IF nParts > 0 THEN
      betweenExtra = leftover / nParts
      leadOffset = betweenExtra / 2
    END IF
  ELSEIF st.justify = Justify.SpaceEvenly THEN
    IF nParts > 0 THEN
      betweenExtra = leftover / (nParts + 1)
      leadOffset = betweenExtra
    END IF
  END IF

  MUT outKids AS List OF Node = []
  MUT curX AS Integer = originX + leadOffset
  MUT crossH AS Integer = 0
  MUT pIdx AS Integer = 0
  MUT placed AS Integer = 0
  FOR EACH k IN kids
    IF isNone(k) THEN
      LET rn AS Sized = layoutNode(k, curX, originY, 0, FALSE, cw, linePx)
      outKids = collections::append(outKids, rn.node)
    ELSE
      ' Main axis: impose the flex-computed outer size so the drawn box exactly
      ' fills the slot the flow reserved (no overlap, no gaps).
      LET fw AS Integer = collections::get(finals, pIdx)
      LET r AS Sized = layoutNode(k, curX, originY, fw, TRUE, cw, linePx)
      outKids = collections::append(outKids, r.node)
      crossH = maxI(crossH, r.height)
      curX = curX + fw + gap
      IF placed < nParts - 1 THEN curX = curX + betweenExtra
      placed = placed + 1
      pIdx = pIdx + 1
    END IF
  NEXT
  RETURN Flow[outKids, contentW, crossH]
END FUNC

' Lay out a container's children. A flex box uses flex layout along its direction;
' everything else (block / inline) uses block formatting with inline runs.
FUNC layoutChildren(kids AS List OF Node, st AS Style, originX AS Integer, originY AS Integer, contentW AS Integer, cw AS Integer, linePx AS Integer) AS Flow
  IF st.display = Display.Flex THEN
    IF st.direction = FlexDirection.Column OR st.direction = FlexDirection.ColumnReverse THEN
      RETURN layoutColumn(kids, st, originX, originY, contentW, cw, linePx)
    END IF
    RETURN layoutRow(kids, st, originX, originY, contentW, cw, linePx)
  END IF
  RETURN layoutBlock(kids, st, originX, originY, contentW, cw, linePx)
END FUNC

' ----- per-node layout ---------------------------------------------------------

' A replaced element renders as a fixed glyph box rather than laying out its
' children. Today that is <textarea> (a multi-line field box); single-line inputs
' and buttons flow inline as decorated text (see gatherText).
FUNC isReplaced(e AS ElementNode) AS Boolean
  RETURN e.tag = "textarea"
END FUNC

' Lay out a replaced element: build the field box as `rows` synthetic text runs of
' |----| (each `cols` dashes wide), size the element to that box plus padding, and
' return it. The element's own children (a textarea's default text) are dropped.
FUNC layoutReplaced(e AS ElementNode, x AS Integer, y AS Integer, cw AS Integer, linePx AS Integer) AS Sized
  LET st AS Style = e.style
  LET ml AS Integer = maxI(0, st.marginLeft)
  LET mr AS Integer = maxI(0, st.marginRight)
  LET mt AS Integer = maxI(0, st.marginTop)
  LET mb AS Integer = maxI(0, st.marginBottom)
  LET pl AS Integer = maxI(0, st.paddingLeft)
  LET pr AS Integer = maxI(0, st.paddingRight)
  LET pt AS Integer = maxI(0, st.paddingTop)
  LET pb AS Integer = maxI(0, st.paddingBottom)
  LET rows AS Integer = attrInt(e.attrs, "rows", 3)
  LET cols AS Integer = attrInt(e.attrs, "cols", 20)
  LET line AS String = "|" & strings::repeat("-", cols) & "|"
  ' The field glyph is (cols + 2) columns wide and `rows` lines tall, in px.
  LET boxW AS Integer = (cols + 2) * cw
  LET contentX AS Integer = x + ml + pl
  LET contentY AS Integer = y + mt + pt
  MUT kids AS List OF Node = []
  MUT i AS Integer = 0
  DO WHILE i < rows
    kids = collections::append(kids, textLaid(line, Layout[contentX, contentY + i * linePx, boxW, linePx], Justify.FlexStart, []))
    i = i + 1
  LOOP
  LET borderW AS Integer = boxW + pl + pr
  LET borderH AS Integer = rows * linePx + pt + pb
  LET box AS Layout = Layout[x + ml, y + mt, borderW, borderH]
  RETURN Sized[elementLaid(e.tag, e.attrs, kids, st, box), borderW + ml + mr, borderH + mt + mb]
END FUNC

' ----- table layout (column-aligned rows and cells) ---------------------------

' A table cell (td/th) and how many columns it spans (colspan).
TYPE Cell
  node AS Node
  span AS Integer
END TYPE

' One table row: its cells, left to right.
TYPE RowCells
  cells AS List OF Cell
END TYPE

' The td/th cells of a <tr>, each with its colspan (default 1).
FUNC collectCells(tr AS ElementNode) AS List OF Cell
  MUT cells AS List OF Cell = []
  FOR EACH c IN tr.children
    MATCH c
      CASE ElementNode(ce)
        IF ce.tag = "td" OR ce.tag = "th" THEN
          cells = collections::append(cells, Cell[c, maxI(1, attrInt(ce.attrs, "colspan", 1))])
        END IF
      CASE ELSE
    END MATCH
  NEXT
  RETURN cells
END FUNC

' Every <tr> of a table in document order, unwrapping <thead>/<tbody>/<tfoot>.
FUNC collectRows(e AS ElementNode) AS List OF RowCells
  MUT rows AS List OF RowCells = []
  FOR EACH c IN e.children
    MATCH c
      CASE ElementNode(ce)
        IF ce.tag = "tr" THEN
          rows = collections::append(rows, RowCells[collectCells(ce)])
        ELSEIF ce.tag = "tbody" OR ce.tag = "thead" OR ce.tag = "tfoot" THEN
          FOR EACH gr IN collectRows(ce)
            rows = collections::append(rows, gr)
          NEXT
        END IF
      CASE ELSE
    END MATCH
  NEXT
  RETURN rows
END FUNC

' The min-content width of a node: its longest unbreakable word (the narrowest a
' column can get without splitting a word). Uses gatherText so a control's glyph
' (|S:--|, [link]) counts as one unbreakable token.
FUNC minContentW(n AS Node, cw AS Integer) AS Integer
  LET t AS String = collapseWs(gatherText(n))
  MUT mx AS Integer = 0
  FOR EACH w IN strings::split(t, " ")
    mx = maxI(mx, strings::displayWidth(w))
  NEXT
  RETURN mx * cw
END FUNC

' Sum of the list entries in [lo, hi).
FUNC sumRange(xs AS List OF Integer, lo AS Integer, hi AS Integer) AS Integer
  MUT total AS Integer = 0
  MUT i AS Integer = lo
  DO WHILE i < hi
    total = total + collections::get(xs, i)
    i = i + 1
  LOOP
  RETURN total
END FUNC

' Lay out a <table> as real columns: each column's width is the max of its cells'
' natural content widths (a spanning cell shares its width across its columns),
' shrunk to fit the available width; every row then places its cells at the shared
' column positions, so columns line up across rows. A cell is laid at its column
' width and a row's height is its tallest cell. The laid cell subtrees are returned
' flattened under the table element — the tr/tbody wrappers carry no visible ink, so
' this is all the painter needs. colspan is honored for width and placement; rowspan
' is treated as a single row (a known limitation).
FUNC layoutTable(e AS ElementNode, x AS Integer, y AS Integer, availW AS Integer, imposeWidth AS Boolean, cw AS Integer, linePx AS Integer) AS Sized
  LET st AS Style = e.style
  LET ml AS Integer = maxI(0, st.marginLeft)
  LET mr AS Integer = maxI(0, st.marginRight)
  LET mt AS Integer = maxI(0, st.marginTop)
  LET mb AS Integer = maxI(0, st.marginBottom)
  LET pl AS Integer = maxI(0, st.paddingLeft)
  LET pr AS Integer = maxI(0, st.paddingRight)
  LET pt AS Integer = maxI(0, st.paddingTop)
  LET pb AS Integer = maxI(0, st.paddingBottom)
  ' The maximum width the table may occupy: an explicit/imposed width, else fill.
  LET explicitW AS Integer = resolveWidthPx(st, availW)
  MUT maxBorderW AS Integer = availW - ml - mr
  IF imposeWidth = FALSE AND explicitW >= 0 THEN maxBorderW = explicitW
  maxBorderW = maxI(0, maxBorderW)
  LET maxContentW AS Integer = maxI(0, maxBorderW - pl - pr)
  LET autoWidth AS Boolean = imposeWidth = FALSE AND explicitW < 0
  LET originX AS Integer = x + ml + pl
  LET originY AS Integer = y + mt + pt

  LET rows AS List OF RowCells = collectRows(e)
  ' Column count: the widest row, summing colspans.
  MUT numCols AS Integer = 0
  FOR EACH r IN rows
    MUT sc AS Integer = 0
    FOR EACH cell IN r.cells
      sc = sc + cell.span
    NEXT
    numCols = maxI(numCols, sc)
  NEXT
  IF numCols = 0 THEN
    LET box0 AS Layout = Layout[x + ml, y + mt, maxBorderW, pt + pb]
    RETURN Sized[elementLaid(e.tag, e.attrs, [], st, box0), maxBorderW + ml + mr, pt + pb + mt + mb]
  END IF

  ' Natural (max-content) and minimum (min-content) column widths. A spanning cell
  ' shares each across the columns it covers.
  MUT colNat AS List OF Integer = []
  MUT colMin AS List OF Integer = []
  MUT ci AS Integer = 0
  DO WHILE ci < numCols
    colNat = collections::append(colNat, 0)
    colMin = collections::append(colMin, 0)
    ci = ci + 1
  LOOP
  FOR EACH r IN rows
    MUT col AS Integer = 0
    FOR EACH cell IN r.cells
      LET perNat AS Integer = (maxContentOuterW(cell.node, cw) + cell.span - 1) / cell.span
      LET perMin AS Integer = (minContentW(cell.node, cw) + cell.span - 1) / cell.span
      MUT j AS Integer = 0
      DO WHILE j < cell.span AND col + j < numCols
        LET idx AS Integer = col + j
        colNat = collections::set(colNat, idx, maxI(collections::get(colNat, idx), perNat))
        colMin = collections::set(colMin, idx, maxI(collections::get(colMin, idx), perMin))
        j = j + 1
      LOOP
      col = col + cell.span
    NEXT
  NEXT

  ' Table width: shrink-to-fit the natural columns when the width is auto (so the
  ' table is narrower than the container and can be centred by an ancestor), else
  ' fill the explicit/imposed width. A one-cell gap (cw px) separates columns.
  LET gap AS Integer = cw
  LET gapsTotal AS Integer = gap * maxI(0, numCols - 1)
  LET naturalContentW AS Integer = sumList(colNat) + gapsTotal
  MUT contentW AS Integer = maxContentW
  IF autoWidth THEN
    contentW = minI(naturalContentW, maxContentW)
  END IF
  LET borderW AS Integer = contentW + pl + pr

  ' Fit the columns to the table's content width. If the natural widths fit, use
  ' them; otherwise shrink each column toward its min-content, distributing the
  ' slack across the columns' flex range (natural - min). A column never shrinks
  ' below its longest word, so a table that cannot fit overflows (and clips at the
  ' edge) rather than corrupting neighbours.
  LET avail AS Integer = maxI(0, contentW - gapsTotal)
  LET totalNat AS Integer = sumList(colNat)
  LET totalMin AS Integer = sumList(colMin)
  MUT colW AS List OF Integer = colNat
  IF totalNat > avail THEN
    IF totalMin >= avail OR totalNat <= totalMin THEN
      colW = colMin
    ELSE
      LET flexTotal AS Integer = totalNat - totalMin
      LET extra AS Integer = avail - totalMin
      MUT scaled AS List OF Integer = []
      MUT used AS Integer = 0
      MUT k AS Integer = 0
      DO WHILE k < numCols
        MUT w AS Integer = 0
        IF k = numCols - 1 THEN
          w = avail - used
        ELSE
          w = collections::get(colMin, k) + (collections::get(colNat, k) - collections::get(colMin, k)) * extra / flexTotal
        END IF
        w = maxI(collections::get(colMin, k), w)
        scaled = collections::append(scaled, w)
        used = used + w
        k = k + 1
      LOOP
      colW = scaled
    END IF
  END IF

  ' Place each row's cells at the shared column positions.
  MUT outKids AS List OF Node = []
  MUT curY AS Integer = originY
  FOR EACH r IN rows
    MUT col AS Integer = 0
    MUT rowH AS Integer = 0
    FOR EACH cell IN r.cells
      LET endCol AS Integer = minI(numCols, col + cell.span)
      LET cellX AS Integer = originX + sumRange(colW, 0, col) + gap * col
      LET cellW AS Integer = sumRange(colW, col, endCol) + gap * maxI(0, endCol - col - 1)
      LET rr AS Sized = layoutNode(cell.node, cellX, curY, maxI(0, cellW), TRUE, cw, linePx)
      outKids = collections::append(outKids, rr.node)
      rowH = maxI(rowH, rr.height)
      col = col + cell.span
    NEXT
    curY = curY + rowH
  NEXT

  LET contentH AS Integer = curY - originY
  MUT borderH AS Integer = contentH + pt + pb
  IF st.height >= 0 THEN borderH = st.height
  borderH = maxI(0, borderH)
  LET box AS Layout = Layout[x + ml, y + mt, borderW, borderH]
  RETURN Sized[elementLaid(e.tag, e.attrs, outKids, st, box), borderW + ml + mr, borderH + mt + mb]
END FUNC

' Lay out one element at (x, y) given the width available to its margin box, and
' return the rebuilt element (with its Layout set) plus its outer size. When
' `imposeWidth` is TRUE the border box is forced to fill the available width less
' margins (a flex row item, whose main size the container already computed);
' otherwise the element self-sizes (explicit width, else fill — block stretch).
FUNC layoutElement(e AS ElementNode, x AS Integer, y AS Integer, availW AS Integer, imposeWidth AS Boolean, cw AS Integer, linePx AS Integer) AS Sized
  LET st AS Style = e.style
  IF st.display = Display.None THEN
    RETURN Sized[elementLaid(e.tag, e.attrs, e.children, st, Layout[x, y, 0, 0]), 0, 0]
  END IF
  IF isReplaced(e) THEN
    RETURN layoutReplaced(e, x, y, cw, linePx)
  END IF
  IF e.tag = "table" THEN
    RETURN layoutTable(e, x, y, availW, imposeWidth, cw, linePx)
  END IF

  LET ml AS Integer = maxI(0, st.marginLeft)
  LET mr AS Integer = maxI(0, st.marginRight)
  LET mt AS Integer = maxI(0, st.marginTop)
  LET mb AS Integer = maxI(0, st.marginBottom)
  LET pl AS Integer = maxI(0, st.paddingLeft)
  LET pr AS Integer = maxI(0, st.paddingRight)
  LET pt AS Integer = maxI(0, st.paddingTop)
  LET pb AS Integer = maxI(0, st.paddingBottom)

  ' Border-box width: imposed → fill available less margins; else the resolved
  ' explicit width (a percent resolves against the containing block = availW); else
  ' fill available less margins.
  LET explicitW AS Integer = resolveWidthPx(st, availW)
  MUT borderW AS Integer = availW - ml - mr
  IF imposeWidth = FALSE AND explicitW >= 0 THEN borderW = explicitW
  borderW = maxI(0, borderW)
  LET contentW AS Integer = maxI(0, borderW - pl - pr)

  LET originX AS Integer = x + ml + pl
  LET originY AS Integer = y + mt + pt
  LET flow AS Flow = layoutChildren(e.children, st, originX, originY, contentW, cw, linePx)

  ' Border-box height: an explicit height, else the content height plus padding.
  MUT borderH AS Integer = flow.usedH + pt + pb
  IF st.height >= 0 THEN borderH = st.height
  borderH = maxI(0, borderH)

  LET box AS Layout = Layout[x + ml, y + mt, borderW, borderH]
  RETURN Sized[elementLaid(e.tag, e.attrs, flow.kids, st, box), borderW + ml + mr, borderH + mt + mb]
END FUNC

' Place a text run at (x, y): its box wraps to the available width, so the box
' width is that wrap width and the height is the resulting line count. The outer
' size the parent flows by is the text's ink width (never wider than available)
' and the same height.
FUNC layoutText(s AS String, x AS Integer, y AS Integer, availW AS Integer, cw AS Integer, linePx AS Integer) AS Sized
  LET h AS Integer = textHeight(s, availW, cw, linePx)
  LET box AS Layout = Layout[x, y, availW, h]
  RETURN Sized[textLaid(s, box, Justify.FlexStart, []), minI(availW, textWidth(s, cw)), h]
END FUNC

' Lay out any node, returning the rebuilt node (with its box set) and its outer
' size.
FUNC layoutNode(n AS Node, x AS Integer, y AS Integer, availW AS Integer, imposeWidth AS Boolean, cw AS Integer, linePx AS Integer) AS Sized
  MUT result AS Sized = Sized[n, 0, 0]
  MATCH n
    CASE ElementNode(e)
      result = layoutElement(e, x, y, availW, imposeWidth, cw, linePx)
    CASE TextNode(t)
      result = layoutText(t.text, x, y, availW, cw, linePx)
    CASE HeaderNode(h)
      result = Sized[n, 0, 0]
    CASE ELSE
  END MATCH
  RETURN result
END FUNC

' Fill in every ElementNode.layout across the document by laying the tree out into a
' `viewportPx`-wide viewport, in device-independent CSS px. `cellPx`/`linePx` are the
' renderer's glyph size in px (how wide/tall one monospace character cell is), used to
' measure and wrap text; a pixel renderer with a real font supplies its own metrics.
' `fitWidth` treats an explicit width wider than the viewport as auto (fill) so a
' desktop-width page reflows to a narrow viewport. Call after resolveStyles(); re-call
' when the viewport or glyph size changes. Returns the rebuilt document (records are
' immutable), every box in CSS px.
EXPORT FUNC updateLayout(doc AS Node, viewportPx AS Integer, cellPx AS Integer, linePx AS Integer, fitWidth AS Boolean) AS Node
  LET vp AS Integer = maxI(0, viewportPx)
  LET cw AS Integer = maxI(1, cellPx)
  LET lh AS Integer = maxI(1, linePx)
  ' Resolve viewport-relative widths to px and apply the fitWidth clamp up front (the
  ' viewport is fixed); percent widths stay tagged and resolve against each containing
  ' block during layout.
  LET tree AS Node = resolveWidths(doc, vp, fitWidth)
  LET r AS Sized = layoutNode(tree, 0, 0, vp, FALSE, cw, lh)
  RETURN r.node
END FUNC
